# Authentication and tenancy

{{ model.manifest.service_name }} signs users in with **Amazon Cognito** using the OAuth 2.0
authorization code flow with **PKCE** (public client, no client secret). The code lives in
`apps/{{ model.manifest.service_name }}/src/app/core/auth/` and is horizontal: product-specific
rules belong in your features, not in these files.

{{ if model.manifest.ui_targets | array.contains "mobile" }}
> **Native mobile:** sign-in is not supported in the Capacitor native shell yet. It fails closed:
> the login page reports that sign-in is unavailable. The app works in a browser as usual.

{{ end }}
## 1. Cognito setup

Create (or reuse) an app client in the user pool:

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Client type | Public client, **no client secret** |
| OAuth grant | Authorization code grant |
| Scopes | `openid` plus what you need (for example `email`, `profile`) |
| Callback URL | `https://<app-host>/auth/callback` (and `http://localhost:4200/auth/callback` for development) |
| Sign-out URL | The `postLogoutRedirectUri` below, for example `https://<app-host>/login` |
| Token revocation | Enabled (the default), so sign-out can revoke the refresh token |

The API must accept this app client: add its ID to the API's `Forge:Oidc:ClientIds`, and allow the
app's origin in the API's `Forge:Cors:AllowedOrigins`.

## 2. Runtime configuration

The app reads `assets/app-config.json` at startup, so one build runs in every environment. Copy
`src/assets/app-config.example.json` to `src/assets/app-config.json` and fill it in. The real file
is ignored by git; deployments provide it (for example a mounted file or a config map).

| Key | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `apiUrl` | API base URL. Access tokens are sent only to this origin and path. https (http only on localhost). |
| `auth.cognitoDomain` | Hosted UI domain as an origin, for example `https://auth.example.com`. |
| `auth.issuer` | `https://cognito-idp.<region>.amazonaws.com/<user-pool-id>` |
| `auth.clientId` | Public app client ID. |
| `auth.scopes` | Array; must contain `openid`. |
| `auth.redirectUri` | This app's `/auth/callback` URL. Must be on the app's origin. |
| `auth.postLogoutRedirectUri` | Where Cognito returns after sign-out. Must be on the app's origin. |
| `auth.roleClaim` | Optional. Access-token claim with the roles. Default `cognito:groups`. |
| `tenancy.mode` | `SingleTenant` or `MultiTenant`: must match the API's `Forge:Tenant:Mode`. |
| `tenancy.claimType` | Optional. Access-token tenant claim. Default `tenant_id` (the API default). |

All of these values are public: the browser downloads them. Cognito domain, pool and client IDs are
identifiers, not secrets. **Never add a client secret**; the app rejects any `auth` key containing
"secret".

**Fail closed.** If the file is missing, unreadable or invalid, sign-in is unavailable, the login page
lists the problems, and no request receives an access token.

## 3. Sign-in

1. "Sign in" creates a random `state`, `nonce` and PKCE `code_verifier`
   (`crypto.getRandomValues`, via [oauth4webapi](https://github.com/panva/oauth4webapi)), stores
   them in `sessionStorage` for this tab, and redirects to Cognito with the S256 code challenge.
2. On `/auth/callback` the stored attempt is removed **before** anything is checked, so a callback
   URL works once. Attempts older than 10 minutes are rejected.
3. The callback must carry the same `state`; Cognito errors (`?error=`) are rejected.
4. The code is exchanged at `/oauth2/token` with the `code_verifier`.
5. The ID token is validated (rules below). Any failure leaves the user signed out.
6. The user continues to the page they originally asked for (app paths only).

### ID token validation rules

Checked by oauth4webapi and `cognito-client.ts`:

- The response is a JSON token response with `access_token` and `token_type` `Bearer`.
- JWS header: `alg` is `RS256`; no `crit` header.
- `iss` equals `auth.issuer` exactly.
- `aud` contains `auth.clientId`; if there are several audiences, `azp` must equal it.
- `exp` is in the future (30 seconds clock tolerance); `iat` and `sub` are present.
- `nonce` equals the nonce sent with this sign-in.
- `token_use` is `id` (Cognito-specific).
- After a refresh, a new ID token must have the same `sub` as the current user.

**Signature exception.** The ID token signature is not verified in the browser. The token comes
directly from Cognito's token endpoint over TLS in response to the app's own request, which
OpenID Connect Core 1.0 §3.1.3.7 allows in place of signature validation. The browser never accepts
an ID token from the URL or from another party.

**Access tokens are not validated by the app.** The app reads their claims (roles, tenant) for display
and navigation only. The API validates every access token (signature, issuer, `token_use=access`,
client ID) and decides what the user may do.

## 4. Tokens, storage and refresh

| Item | Where |
|---|---|
| Access token | Memory only |
| Refresh token | `sessionStorage` of this tab (`pervaxis.auth.refresh_token`) |
| ID token | Not stored; its validated claims (name, email) are kept in memory |

**XSS tradeoff.** `sessionStorage` lets the session survive a page reload in the same tab, but any
script running on the page (an XSS flaw or a compromised dependency) can read the refresh token and
keep obtaining access tokens until it is revoked or expires. Keeping it in memory only would remove
that exposure at the cost of a new sign-in after every reload. Mitigations in place: a strict Content
Security Policy (`script-src 'self'`), no `innerHTML` bindings in the scaffold, sign-out revokes the
refresh token, and the token is scoped to one tab and cleared when the tab closes. Keep the Cognito
refresh-token lifetime as short as your users accept.

**Refresh.**

- One refresh at a time: the expiry timer, API 401s and the reload restore share a single request.
- Access tokens are refreshed 60 seconds before they expire (or halfway through very short lifetimes).
- After a reload the session is restored with that shared refresh; protected routes wait for it.
- A failed refresh ends the session in this tab and returns to the login page.
- Cognito calls (token, refresh, revoke) use `fetch`, not Angular's `HttpClient`, so the auth, retry
  and error interceptors never see them.

**API requests.** `authInterceptor` adds `Authorization: Bearer` only when the parsed request URL has
the same origin as `apiUrl` and its path is at or below the `apiUrl` path. Look-alike hosts,
protocol-relative URLs, other schemes or ports, URLs with credentials and sibling paths
(`/apiv2` for `/api`) never receive the token.

**401 handling.** The token is refreshed (shared) and the request is replayed **at most once**:

- `GET`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`: replayed.
- Writes (`POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`): replayed only when the 401 carries
  `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"`. The generated API's JWT bearer handler sends this
  when it rejects the token during authentication, before any endpoint code runs. Any other 401 is
  returned to the caller, because the request may already have had an effect. Do not return this
  challenge from endpoint code after making changes.
- If the API is on another origin, the browser hides `WWW-Authenticate` unless the API exposes it
  (CORS `Access-Control-Expose-Headers: WWW-Authenticate`). Without that, writes are not replayed;
  the token is still refreshed, so the user's next attempt succeeds.

The generic retry interceptor retries only `GET`, `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` to the API on 5xx or network
errors.

## 5. Sign-out

1. Tokens, the user profile, the tenant and all registered user/tenant state are cleared **immediately**.
2. Other tabs are told to sign out (`BroadcastChannel`, or `localStorage` events as a fallback).
   A tab that receives the message signs out locally and never broadcasts again, so there are no loops.
3. The refresh token is revoked at `/oauth2/revoke` (best effort, `keepalive`). The app waits at most
   2 seconds for it.
4. The browser goes to Cognito `/logout`, which ends the hosted UI session and returns to
   `postLogoutRedirectUri`.

After 15 minutes without activity, a signed-in user is signed out the same way.

## 6. Roles and permissions

Roles come from the access token's `cognito:groups` claim (configurable). Use them to show or hide UI:

```typescript
canActivate: [authGuard, permissionGuard('Admin')]
```

`AuthService.hasPermission()` maps permissions one-to-one to groups. These checks are for the UI
only; the API enforces the same rules.

## 7. Tenancy

The tenant is read from the access token's `tenant_id` claim and must be exactly one GUID string,
the same rule the API applies. V1 supports **one tenant per user**: there is no tenant switcher, and
`TenantContextService.canSwitchTenant` is `false`.

| `tenancy.mode` | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `SingleTenant` | The tenant claim is ignored (the API uses its own tenant). Users without a tenant may use the app. |
| `MultiTenant` | Routes with `tenantGuard` require a valid tenant claim; users without one see "Access denied". Leave `tenantGuard` off pages the API serves without a tenant (for example platform administration). |

Memberships come from `TENANT_MEMBERSHIP_SOURCE`. The default source returns the token's tenant.
A future membership lookup can be provided there without changing the rest of the app.

**Clear tenant data.** Stores that hold user or tenant data register a reset listener; it runs on
sign-out, when the session ends and when the token's tenant changes:

```typescript
constructor() {
  inject(SessionStateRegistry).registerUntilDestroyed(inject(DestroyRef), () => this.items.set([]));
}
```

The API remains responsible for validating tenant access on every request.

## 8. Content Security Policy

`index.html` allows `connect-src` to any https origin (plus localhost) because the Cognito domain and
API URL are runtime settings. In production, also send a CSP header from the web server with
`connect-src 'self' <cognitoDomain> <api origin>`; browsers enforce both policies, so the header
tightens the meta tag.

## 9. Tests

`npm test` runs the auth specs: callback state and nonce checks, one-time callbacks, refresh success,
failure and concurrency, session restore, sign-out across tabs, token destinations, write replay
rules, permissions, single-tenant and multi-tenant claims, and state cleanup.
